%% md
# The hand-swap bug: measuring train-serve skew in MediaPipe sign recognition

{{HEADER}}

A sign classifier can lose most of its accuracy in a live app while the model file stays the same, because the live pipeline feeds it slightly different numbers than training did. ManuVox hit this: its first browser build put each detected hand in the other hand's slot. On the author's 50 webcam clips, fixing that one mapping raised clip top-1 from 10% to 46% in the Python pipeline and from 16% to 41% in the browser (`results/live_gap_v2.md` in the repository). This notebook trains the model from notebook 4 on a shorter schedule, then replays six skews on four held-out signers: swapped hand slots, a mirrored camera, flipped coordinates, other camera aspect ratios, lower frame rates and dropped frames. It measures the accuracy each one costs and tests a fix for two of them.

Data: the `asl-signs` competition files only (CC BY 4.0). Needs a GPU (T4), about 30 minutes on Kaggle. Part 2 of a 5-notebook series.

Contents

1. Setup and data
2. Train on clean data
3. The skews as functions
4. What each skew costs
5. Fixing the hand slots
6. Aspect ratio: a sweep and a self-calibrating fix
7. Frame rate and dropped frames
8. What we found

%% md
## 1. Setup and data

The readers and the preprocessing are the same as in notebook 1. Preprocessing drops frames without a hand, mirrors left-dominant sequences, centres on the shoulders, divides by the shoulder width and resamples 32 frames.

